{"text":"Recent work supports separating directed purchase relations from general similarity. [TransGAT (2024)](https://www.amazon.science/publications/transitivity-encoded-graph-attention-networks-for-complementary-item-recommendations) encodes behavioral direction and jointly learns complement/substitute graphs; its supervision requirements exceed unlabeled interaction histories. [Revisiting Graph Projections (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09209) offers a simpler precedent: a directed weighted graph projected from user–item interactions for complementary recommendation. These results motivate asymmetric relations, but observed purchase transitions alone do not establish functional complementarity or product compatibility.

For sparse sequential prediction, [GaGNN (2024)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S002002552400536X) combines group and individual transition graphs, while [LS4SRec (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3723173) uses a global behavior graph alongside separate long- and short-term interest encoders. Both support pooling transition evidence across users rather than relying exclusively on each short history. Their reported findings do not establish gains on the pinned RecHarness benchmark.

Practical inference for this task: retain SASRec and add a small directed transition residual, using separate source/target embeddings, frequency smoothing, and bounded outgoing neighborhoods built exclusively from training histories. Encode available titles/descriptions with randomly initialized token embeddings plus mean pooling or a small CNN, trained jointly on next-item prediction; treat this as a proposed adaptation, not a verified recipe from these papers. Gate text contributions when metadata is missing or weak, and keep compatibility unknown without explicit textual evidence. Compare transition-only, text-only, and combined variants using macro validation HR@10; no reviewed source establishes that a text encoder trained from scratch will improve this setting."}
